{
  "units": [
    {
      "title": "Interest‑Rate Differential Effects on Exchange Rate",
      "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0024", "CON_0023"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0024", "CON_0023"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": null,
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0024",
              "exact_quote": "If the domestic interest rate is above that, the international interest rate, then the exchange rate today has to be above the expected exchange rate, because that will give you a expected depreciation of the currency, which will compensate for the fact that the domestic bond is paying a higher interest rate than international bond."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0023",
              "exact_quote": "Conversely, if the domestic bond is paying a lower interest rate, then the exchange rate today is very depreciated, because you have to expect it to appreciate in order to compensate for the interest rate differential."
            }
          ],
          "payload": {
            "type": "table",
            "title": "Interest‑Rate Differential Effects on Exchange Rate",
            "headers": ["Domestic Interest Rate vs. Foreign", "Relation of Today’s Exchange Rate to Expected", "Implied Currency Movement"],
            "rows": [
              ["Higher than foreign", "Exchange rate today > expected exchange rate", "Expected depreciation of domestic currency"],
              ["Lower than foreign", "Exchange rate today < expected exchange rate (depreciated)", "Expected appreciation of domestic currency"]
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Monetary‑Policy Transmission in an Open Economy",
      "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0028", "CON_0036", "CON_0037", "CON_0038"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0028", "CON_0036", "CON_0037", "CON_0038"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": null,
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0028",
              "exact_quote": "An increase in the interest rate is contractionary for two reasons: it reduces investment and it appreciates the exchange rate, which lowers net exports."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0036",
              "exact_quote": "An expansionary domestic monetary policy will lead to a contraction in output and an appreciation of the currency."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0037",
              "exact_quote": "An expansionary domestic monetary policy will lead to a contraction in output."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0038",
              "exact_quote": "An expansionary domestic monetary policy will lead to an appreciation of the currency."
            }
          ],
